#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124286 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124626 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f015a46e-7493-558d-a1d8-c2d90f9306ab" :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Odd Power"}] :question/id #uuid "1fd5312f-c992-5218-bcfd-9bc235e9cd53" :question/math-academy-id "e-41" :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} \\,$." :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n1. Take the graph of $y = \\dfrac 1 {x^3}$.\n2. Translate it to the left by $1$ unit. This gives $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/24/2497877ff3bc4a10871103f04502252843e2bdf8a9460c9ae4b32ff5344e921a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^-)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} = -\\infty, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^+)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} = \\infty\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3}= \\textrm{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124289 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124627 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "038fb60a-e596-5e48-b05e-f8414d454518" :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Even Power"}] :question/id #uuid "08d460c2-32e7-5e20-86cf-4a1c2bf13c28" :question/math-academy-id "e-43" :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits {x\\rightarrow -1} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4}$." :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n1. Take the graph of $y = \\dfrac 1 {x^4}$.\n2. Translate it to the left by $1$ unit. This gives $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/99f04a25115f084e40e9f8f9b06a6749532a2ba2320ee23d31af050a87957269.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^-)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} = \\infty, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^+)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} = \\infty\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4}= \\infty\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124386 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124573 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d13b888-0ef7-5194-a54d-435d9b344749" :knowledge-point/title "Limits at Infinity of Reciprocal Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "ca99dd0a-e2bc-5b6a-8138-ffaac453a0d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-773" :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{4x^4}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 4\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1}{4x^{4}} &= lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x^{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1}{x^{4}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^4}\\right)=0\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^4} = \\dfrac 1 4\\cdot 0 = 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124387 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124575 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b50ced1a-50ab-57f3-a60d-dd1d9d5bd997" :knowledge-point/title "Limits at Infinity of Reciprocals of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "96ecee6e-f551-5ecd-9991-0f962190f382" :question/math-academy-id "e-774" :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{(1-x)^4} \\,$." :question/worked-solution "For any non-constant polynomial $P(x)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{P(x)}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $(1-x)^4$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{(1-x)^4} = 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124627 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "038fb60a-e596-5e48-b05e-f8414d454518" :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a39ec0dc-20a4-50a6-871b-a8bc6f99aaa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123561} {:answer/id #uuid "a4224f6c-5e46-5f88-bb3c-a313cd1cfdd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∞" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123562} {:answer/id #uuid "25f202d5-26c0-544b-af9c-7de695d63ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123563} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8b15b5-04e7-56f5-889a-8191d462785d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123564} {:answer/id #uuid "66b95ae4-9367-5f11-8a17-b0ca145fc2e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ∞" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123565}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8745453d-9410-5078-9c89-dc59dd94a1c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123560}] :question/id #uuid "e5471135-7221-5e32-8634-6bbbec676d01" :question/math-academy-id "q-46972" :question/problem "Compute $lim_(x → 1)(1)/((x - 1)^{2})$."}]}
